From d53f850146a5a8122afbbc54191035da68916747 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: "Jonathan D.A. Jewell" <6759885+hyperpolymath@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Mon, 9 Nov 2026 10:05:21 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 1/2] test(differential): exercise the native adapter against a
 stand-in backend
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Codecov reported this branch at 52.38% patch coverage with 30 lines missing,
all of them in `src/analysis/differential.jl`: the native adapter — the block
that turns a CompositionalDA `DAResult` into result rows, keeps BH from being
applied a second time, and maps structural-zero verdicts — was unreachable in
CI. The reason is real and not going away: CompositionalDA is not in the General
registry, so `Pkg.instantiate()` cannot install it and no checkout CI can
produce has the package. Only the refusal path was measured, because only the
refusal path could run.

The fix is a seam, not a lower bar:

- `differential_abundance(...; native_backend=…)` calls a module rather than the
  package name. `nothing` (the default) means the `CompositionalDA` this session
  loaded, or the existing by-name refusal when it did not; a module passed in is
  used as given. Production behaviour is unchanged.
- `_is_composition_error` translates the backend's own `CompositionError`, so a
  stand-in's error type is translated exactly as the package's is.
- `diagnostics.native_method.package` now reports `nameof(backend)` instead of
  the literal "CompositionalDA". A record made with a stand-in must not read as
  one made with the package; this is the field that says which produced it.

`test/support/native_da_stub.jl` is a stand-in with the real package's two entry
points, column names and verdict strings ("tested", "structural_zero",
"structural_zero_both"), and deliberately simple arithmetic on the feature's
position so every assertion can name the number it expects. Its `qvalue` is not
`bh_adjust(pvalue)`, so a second BH pass cannot pass unnoticed, and its
structural verdicts carry NaN exactly as the package leaves them, so the adapter
must branch on the verdict before it looks at finiteness.

`test/unit/test_differential_native.jl` gains four testsets that run everywhere:
the seam itself, the ALDEx2 wiring (including the BH suppression and the group
relabelling that makes the contrast direction the user asked for), the ANCOM-BC
wiring (units, structural decisions, filtered taxa), and the refusals in their
order (input checks before the dependency probe, `CompositionError` translated,
other failures rethrown untouched, non-finite statistics refused by name). The
real-package comparisons stay where they are: they are stronger evidence when
the package is developed into the environment, and they still skip loudly when
it is not.

